Dear Fabbaloo Editors,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 has been released. The local desktop application started as a
way to adapt downloaded 3D-printing models without rebuilding them in CAD; the
new release connects that edit to reusable design components and material-aware
slicer handoff.

The clearest demonstration begins with an imported STL whose hole is wrong.
Solidon changes the recognised hole on its original axis as a reversible
operation. A user can then add a standards-based insert or bearing seat and
apply fit values measured from a printed calibration piece.

Three additions since the first public demo show how the workflow has grown:

* history steps can become a reusable parametric component, with named fields,
  a preview and a tested value range, without carrying executable code;
* matching printable screws and nuts, bearing seats, countersinks and washers
  are available as print-oriented components;
* every physical filament spool can carry colour and process settings into the
  correct 3MF material slot and on to the external slicer.

The release also improves in-viewport constrained sketching and adds an
AppImage alongside Flatpak, Windows and macOS packages. Projects remain local;
AI is an optional control layer and is not needed for modelling or export.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Release history: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can send a compact story pack with the 0.2.2 build, a measured STL edit,
three high-resolution images and a one-page fact sheet.

